{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Dpbz1m97jsqoBBNcJ15wnJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Rust translation of the C library in `c_src/`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Public ABI (exactly matches `nm -D` on the C shared object):\n4\t//!   * `tfm`\n5\t//!\n6\t//! # Bit-exactness\n7\t//!\n8\t//! `tfm` is pure `float` arithmetic, so byte-identical output means matching\n9\t//! not only the IEEE-754 values but also the **NaN payloads** the C build\n10\t//! produces. Two hardware details make those payloads sensitive to how the C\n11\t//! was compiled:\n12\t//!\n13\t//! 1. On x86 SSE, a binary op such as `addss %src, %dst` whose operands are\n14\t//!    *both* NaN returns the (quieted) **destination** operand. Which C operand\n15\t//!    lands in the destination register is a codegen choice — `fadd`/`fmul` are\n16\t//!    commutative for values, so a compiler may freely swap them.\n17\t//! 2. When an invalid operation manufactures a NaN out of non-NaN operands\n18\t//!    (`inf - inf`, `0 * inf`, `sqrt(-x)`), x86 yields the \"indefinite\" QNaN\n19\t//!    `0xffc00000` — note the **set sign bit** — not `0x7fc00000`.\n20\t//!\n21\t//! Consequently `gcc` itself emits three mutually different payload behaviours\n22\t//! at `-O0`, `-O1`/`-O2`/`-Os`, and `-O3`. The reference targeted here is the\n23\t//! library exactly as `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` builds it: that file sets no\n24\t//! `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` and adds no optimization flags, so the reference is the\n25\t//! unoptimized build. Every *numeric* result is identical across all `-O`\n26\t//! levels; the levels differ only in NaN payloads, which are reachable only\n27\t//! when an input is NaN or when squaring an input overflows to infinity.\n28\t//!\n29\t//! To pin this down instead of trusting the optimizer, each\n30\t//! payload-sensitive operation goes through the `f*` helpers below, which model\n31\t//! x86 SSE propagation on raw bit patterns, in the operand order the reference\n32\t//! build emits. That order is plain C source order except for two spots, both\n33\t//! marked in `step`:\n34\t//!\n35\t//! * `2.0f * dx2` is emitted as `dx2 + dx2` (`addss`, destination `dx2`);\n36\t//! * the final `+ (4.0f * dxy * dxy)` is emitted **commuted**, with the\n37\t//!   `4*dxy*dxy` term as the destination, so its payload wins over the\n38\t//!   accumulator's.\n39\t//!\n40\t//! Because the helpers never let a NaN reach a hardware FP instruction, the\n41\t//! output is identical on any host, not just x86.\n42\t//!\n43\t//! No bugs are fixed: the inlined `max(0, sqd)` keeps its C semantics (it\n44\t//! propagates NaN and does not normalize `-0.0`, unlike `f32::max`), the\n45\t//! expressions keep their C evaluation order, and a non-positive `count`\n46\t//! still writes nothing.\n47\t\n48\t#![allow(clippy::missing_safety_doc)]\n49\t\n50\tuse core::ffi::c_int;\n51\t\n52\t/// The x86 \"real indefinite\" QNaN for `f32`, produced when an invalid\n53\t/// operation manufactures a NaN out of non-NaN operands.\n54\tconst INDEFINITE: u32 = 0xffc0_0000;\n55\t\n56\t/// Quiet bit of an `f32` significand.\n57\tconst QUIET_BIT: u32 = 0x0040_0000;\n58\t\n59\tconst ZERO: u32 = 0x0000_0000; // 0.0f\n60\tconst FOUR: u32 = 0x4080_0000; // 4.0f\n61\tconst HALF: u32 = 0x3f00_0000; // 0.5f\n62\t\n63\t#[inline(always)]\n64\tfn is_nan_bits(x: u32) -> bool {\n65\t    (x & 0x7f80_0000) == 0x7f80_0000 && (x & 0x007f_ffff) != 0\n66\t}\n67\t\n68\t/// x86 quieting of a NaN operand: set the quiet bit, preserving the sign and\n69\t/// the rest of the payload. An already-quiet NaN passes through unchanged.\n70\t#[inline(always)]\n71\tfn quiet(x: u32) -> u32 {\n72\t    x | QUIET_BIT\n73\t}\n74\t\n75\t/// x86 SSE NaN propagation for a two-operand op: `a` is the *destination*\n76\t/// operand, so its payload takes precedence. `None` if no operand is NaN.\n77\t#[inline(always)]\n78\tfn nan_result(a: u32, b: u32) -> Option<u32> {\n79\t    if is_nan_bits(a) {\n80\t        Some(quiet(a))\n81\t    } else if is_nan_bits(b) {\n82\t        Some(quiet(b))\n83\t    } else {\n84\t        None\n85\t    }\n86\t}\n87\t\n88\t/// Maps a NaN freshly manufactured from non-NaN operands onto the x86\n89\t/// indefinite QNaN.\n90\t#[inline(always)]\n91\tfn fresh(v: f32) -> u32 {\n92\t    let bits = v.to_bits();\n93\t    if is_nan_bits(bits) {\n94\t        INDEFINITE\n95\t    } else {\n96\t        bits\n97\t    }\n98\t}\n99\t\n100\t/// `addss %b, %a` — `a + b`, with `a` as the destination operand.\n101\t#[inline(always)]\n102\tfn fadd(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32 {\n103\t    match nan_result(a, b) {\n104\t        Some(r) => r,\n105\t        None => fresh(f32::from_bits(a) + f32::from_bits(b)),\n106\t    }\n107\t}\n108\t\n109\t/// `subss %b, %a` — `a - b`, with `a` as the destination operand.\n110\t#[inline(always)]\n111\tfn fsub(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32 {\n112\t    match nan_result(a, b) {\n113\t        Some(r) => r,\n114\t        None => fresh(f32::from_bits(a) - f32::from_bits(b)),\n115\t    }\n116\t}\n117\t\n118\t/// `mulss %b, %a` — `a * b`, with `a` as the destination operand.\n119\t#[inline(always)]\n120\tfn fmul(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32 {\n121\t    match nan_result(a, b) {\n122\t        Some(r) => r,\n123\t        None => fresh(f32::from_bits(a) * f32::from_bits(b)),\n124\t    }\n125\t}\n126\t\n127\t/// `sqrtf` — quiets a NaN operand, yields the indefinite QNaN for a negative\n128\t/// operand, and leaves `-0.0` as `-0.0`.\n129\t#[inline(always)]\n130\tfn fsqrt(a: u32) -> u32 {\n131\t    if is_nan_bits(a) {\n132\t        return quiet(a);\n133\t    }\n134\t    let v = f32::from_bits(a);\n135\t    if v < 0.0 {\n136\t        return INDEFINITE;\n137\t    }\n138\t    v.sqrt().to_bits()\n139\t}\n140\t\n141\t/// `comiss`-based ordered `<`: false whenever either operand is NaN.\n142\t#[inline(always)]\n143\tfn flt(a: u32, b: u32) -> bool {\n144\t    !is_nan_bits(a) && !is_nan_bits(b) && f32::from_bits(a) < f32::from_bits(b)\n145\t}\n146\t\n147\t/// The body shared by both arms of the C `if`, parameterised exactly as the C\n148\t/// source names it. Returns `lambda`.\n149\t#[inline(always)]\n150\tfn lambda(dx2: u32, dy2: u32, dxy: u32) -> u32 {\n151\t    // float sqd = (dy2 * dy2) - (2.0f * dx2 * dy2) + (dx2 * dx2)\n152\t    //             + (4.0f * dxy * dxy);\n153\t    //\n154\t    // `2.0f * dx2` is emitted as the self-addition `dx2 + dx2`.\n155\t    let two_dx2_dy2 = fmul(fadd(dx2, dx2), dy2);\n156\t    let mut sqd = fsub(fmul(dy2, dy2), two_dx2_dy2);\n157\t    sqd = fadd(sqd, fmul(dx2, dx2));\n158\t    // The reference build emits this last addition commuted, with the\n159\t    // `4.0f * dxy * dxy` term as the destination operand.\n160\t    sqd = fadd(fmul(fmul(FOUR, dxy), dxy), sqd);\n161\t\n162\t    // float lambda = 0.5f * (dy2 + dx2 + sqrtf((((0) > (sqd)) ? (0) : (sqd))));\n163\t    //\n164\t    // The ternary is *not* `f32::max`: for NaN `0 > sqd` is false, so the NaN\n165\t    // survives, and `-0.0` is likewise passed through unnormalized.\n166\t    let clamped = if flt(sqd, ZERO) { ZERO } else { sqd };\n167\t    fmul(HALF, fadd(fadd(dy2, dx2), fsqrt(clamped)))\n168\t}\n169\t\n170\t/// One iteration of the C loop: consumes three input `float`s, produces two.\n171\t#[inline(always)]\n172\tfn step(s0: u32, s1: u32, s2: u32) -> (u32, u32) {\n173\t    if flt(s0, s1) {\n174\t        // if (src[0] < src[1])\n175\t        let (dx2, dy2, dxy) = (s0, s1, s2);\n176\t        let l = lambda(dx2, dy2, dxy);\n177\t        // dest[0] = dx2 - lambda;  dest[1] = dxy;\n178\t        (fsub(dx2, l), dxy)\n179\t    } else {\n180\t        let (dy2, dx2, dxy) = (s0, s1, s2);\n181\t        let l = lambda(dx2, dy2, dxy);\n182\t        // dest[0] = dxy;  dest[1] = dx2 - lambda;\n183\t        (dxy, fsub(dx2, l))\n184\t    }\n185\t}\n186\t\n187\t/// ```c\n188\t/// void tfm(float *dest, const float *src, int count);\n189\t/// ```\n190\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n191\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn tfm(dest: *mut f32, src: *const f32, count: c_int) {\n192\t    // Operate on raw bit patterns so that no NaN payload can be laundered by\n193\t    // an incidental hardware move or arithmetic instruction.\n194\t    let src = src as *const u32;\n195\t    let dest = dest as *mut u32;\n196\t\n197\t    let mut i: c_int = 0;\n198\t    while i < count {\n199\t        let inp = 3isize * i as isize;\n200\t        let s0 = src.offset(inp).read_unaligned();\n201\t        let s1 = src.offset(inp + 1).read_unaligned();\n202\t        let s2 = src.offset(inp + 2).read_unaligned();\n203\t\n204\t        let (d0, d1) = step(s0, s1, s2);\n205\t\n206\t        let out = 2isize * i as isize;\n207\t        dest.offset(out).write_unaligned(d0);\n208\t        dest.offset(out + 1).write_unaligned(d1);\n209\t\n210\t        i += 1;\n211\t    }\n212\t}\n213\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"188fe3ed-21de-4d11-9f80-120acb59131f","uuid":"e76af90e-7c2c-4252-9531-4ba709c3fc6b","timestamp":"2026-08-19T14:46:09.230Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-tEkLcs/translated_rust/src/lib.rs","content":"//! Rust translation of the C library in `c_src/`.\n//!\n//! Public ABI (exactly matches `nm -D` on the C shared object):\n//!   * `tfm`\n//!\n//! # Bit-exactness\n//!\n//! `tfm` is pure `float` arithmetic, so byte-identical output means matching\n//! not only the IEEE-754 values but also the **NaN payloads** the C build\n//! produces. Two hardware details make those payloads sensitive to how the C\n//! was compiled:\n//!\n//! 1. On x86 SSE, a binary op such as `addss %src, %dst` whose operands are\n//!    *both* NaN returns the (quieted) **destination** operand. Which C operand\n//!    lands in the destination register is a codegen choice — `fadd`/`fmul` are\n//!    commutative for values, so a compiler may freely swap them.\n//! 2. When an invalid operation manufactures a NaN out of non-NaN operands\n//!    (`inf - inf`, `0 * inf`, `sqrt(-x)`), x86 yields the \"indefinite\" QNaN\n//!    `0xffc00000` — note the **set sign bit** — not `0x7fc00000`.\n//!\n//! Consequently `gcc` itself emits three mutually different payload behaviours\n//! at `-O0`, `-O1`/`-O2`/`-Os`, and `-O3`. The reference targeted here is the\n//! library exactly as `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` builds it: that file sets no\n//! `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` and adds no optimization flags, so the reference is the\n//! unoptimized build. Every *numeric* result is identical across all `-O`\n//! levels; the levels differ only in NaN payloads, which are reachable only\n//! when an input is NaN or when squaring an input overflows to infinity.\n//!\n//! To pin this down instead of trusting the optimizer, each\n//! payload-sensitive operation goes through the `f*` helpers below, which model\n//! x86 SSE propagation on raw bit patterns, in the operand order the reference\n//! build emits. That order is plain C source order except for two spots, both\n//! marked in `step`:\n//!\n//! * `2.0f * dx2` is emitted as `dx2 + dx2` (`addss`, destination `dx2`);\n//! * the final `+ (4.0f * dxy * dxy)` is emitted **commuted**, with the\n//!   `4*dxy*dxy` term as the destination, so its payload wins over the\n//!   accumulator's.\n//!\n//! Because the helpers never let a NaN reach a hardware FP instruction, the\n//! output is identical on any host, not just x86.\n//!\n//! No bugs are fixed: the inlined `max(0, sqd)` keeps its C semantics (it\n//! propagates NaN and does not normalize `-0.0`, unlike `f32::max`), the\n//! expressions keep their C evaluation order, and a non-positive `count`\n//! still writes nothing.\n\n#![allow(clippy::missing_safety_doc)]\n\nuse core::ffi::c_int;\n\n/// The x86 \"real indefinite\" QNaN for `f32`, produced when an invalid\n/// operation manufactures a NaN out of non-NaN operands.\nconst INDEFINITE: u32 = 0xffc0_0000;\n\n/// Quiet bit of an `f32` significand.\nconst QUIET_BIT: u32 = 0x0040_0000;\n\nconst ZERO: u32 = 0x0000_0000; // 0.0f\nconst FOUR: u32 = 0x4080_0000; // 4.0f\nconst HALF: u32 = 0x3f00_0000; // 0.5f\n\n#[inline(always)]\nfn is_nan_bits(x: u32) -> bool {\n    (x & 0x7f80_0000) == 0x7f80_0000 && (x & 0x007f_ffff) != 0\n}\n\n/// x86 quieting of a NaN operand: set the quiet bit, preserving the sign and\n/// the rest of the payload. An already-quiet NaN passes through unchanged.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn quiet(x: u32) -> u32 {\n    x | QUIET_BIT\n}\n\n/// x86 SSE NaN propagation for a two-operand op: `a` is the *destination*\n/// operand, so its payload takes precedence. `None` if no operand is NaN.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn nan_result(a: u32, b: u32) -> Option<u32> {\n    if is_nan_bits(a) {\n        Some(quiet(a))\n    } else if is_nan_bits(b) {\n        Some(quiet(b))\n    } else {\n        None\n    }\n}\n\n/// Maps a NaN freshly manufactured from non-NaN operands onto the x86\n/// indefinite QNaN.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn fresh(v: f32) -> u32 {\n    let bits = v.to_bits();\n    if is_nan_bits(bits) {\n        INDEFINITE\n    } else {\n        bits\n    }\n}\n\n/// `addss %b, %a` — `a + b`, with `a` as the destination operand.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn fadd(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32 {\n    match nan_result(a, b) {\n        Some(r) => r,\n        None => fresh(f32::from_bits(a) + f32::from_bits(b)),\n    }\n}\n\n/// `subss %b, %a` — `a - b`, with `a` as the destination operand.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn fsub(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32 {\n    match nan_result(a, b) {\n        Some(r) => r,\n        None => fresh(f32::from_bits(a) - f32::from_bits(b)),\n    }\n}\n\n/// `mulss %b, %a` — `a * b`, with `a` as the destination operand.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn fmul(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32 {\n    match nan_result(a, b) {\n        Some(r) => r,\n        None => fresh(f32::from_bits(a) * f32::from_bits(b)),\n    }\n}\n\n/// `sqrtf` — quiets a NaN operand, yields the indefinite QNaN for a negative\n/// operand, and leaves `-0.0` as `-0.0`.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn fsqrt(a: u32) -> u32 {\n    if is_nan_bits(a) {\n        return quiet(a);\n    }\n    let v = f32::from_bits(a);\n    if v < 0.0 {\n        return INDEFINITE;\n    }\n    v.sqrt().to_bits()\n}\n\n/// `comiss`-based ordered `<`: false whenever either operand is NaN.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn flt(a: u32, b: u32) -> bool {\n    !is_nan_bits(a) && !is_nan_bits(b) && f32::from_bits(a) < f32::from_bits(b)\n}\n\n/// The body shared by both arms of the C `if`, parameterised exactly as the C\n/// source names it. Returns `lambda`.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn lambda(dx2: u32, dy2: u32, dxy: u32) -> u32 {\n    // float sqd = (dy2 * dy2) - (2.0f * dx2 * dy2) + (dx2 * dx2)\n    //             + (4.0f * dxy * dxy);\n    //\n    // `2.0f * dx2` is emitted as the self-addition `dx2 + dx2`.\n    let two_dx2_dy2 = fmul(fadd(dx2, dx2), dy2);\n    let mut sqd = fsub(fmul(dy2, dy2), two_dx2_dy2);\n    sqd = fadd(sqd, fmul(dx2, dx2));\n    // The reference build emits this last addition commuted, with the\n    // `4.0f * dxy * dxy` term as the destination operand.\n    sqd = fadd(fmul(fmul(FOUR, dxy), dxy), sqd);\n\n    // float lambda = 0.5f * (dy2 + dx2 + sqrtf((((0) > (sqd)) ? (0) : (sqd))));\n    //\n    // The ternary is *not* `f32::max`: for NaN `0 > sqd` is false, so the NaN\n    // survives, and `-0.0` is likewise passed through unnormalized.\n    let clamped = if flt(sqd, ZERO) { ZERO } else { sqd };\n    fmul(HALF, fadd(fadd(dy2, dx2), fsqrt(clamped)))\n}\n\n/// One iteration of the C loop: consumes three input `float`s, produces two.\n#[inline(always)]\nfn step(s0: u32, s1: u32, s2: u32) -> (u32, u32) {\n    if flt(s0, s1) {\n        // if (src[0] < src[1])\n        let (dx2, dy2, dxy) = (s0, s1, s2);\n        let l = lambda(dx2, dy2, dxy);\n        // dest[0] = dx2 - lambda;  dest[1] = dxy;\n        (fsub(dx2, l), dxy)\n    } else {\n        let (dy2, dx2, dxy) = (s0, s1, s2);\n        let l = lambda(dx2, dy2, dxy);\n        // dest[0] = dxy;  dest[1] = dx2 - lambda;\n        (dxy, fsub(dx2, l))\n    }\n}\n\n/// ```c\n/// void tfm(float *dest, const float *src, int count);\n/// ```\n#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\npub unsafe extern \"C\" fn tfm(dest: *mut f32, src: *const f32, count: c_int) {\n    // Operate on raw bit patterns so that no NaN payload can be laundered by\n    // an incidental hardware move or arithmetic instruction.\n    let src = src as *const u32;\n    let dest = dest as *mut u32;\n\n    let mut i: c_int = 0;\n    while i < count {\n        let inp = 3isize * i as isize;\n        let s0 = src.offset(inp).read_unaligned();\n        let s1 = src.offset(inp + 1).read_unaligned();\n        let s2 = src.offset(inp + 2).read_unaligned();\n\n        let (d0, d1) = step(s0, s1, s2);\n\n        let out = 2isize * i as isize;\n        dest.offset(out).write_unaligned(d0);\n        dest.offset(out + 1).write_unaligned(d1);\n\n        i += 1;\n    }\n}\n","numLines":213,"startLine":1,"totalLines":213}}}
