{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_015xy2xw9PX5qBGefy6xwHhU","type":"tool_result","content":"=== Cargo.toml ===\n[package]\nname = \"translated_rust\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n[lib]\nname = \"tfm_lib\"\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\npath = \"src/lib.rs\"\n\n[features]\ndefault = []\n\n[dev-dependencies]\nlibloading = \"0.8\"\n\n[profile.release]\npanic = \"abort\"\n\n[workspace]\n\n=== deliverables ===\nCONFIGS.md\nERRORS.md\nSYMBOLS.md\nVERIFICATION.md\nmutation_check.sh\ntests/common/mod.rs\ntests/fuzz_explore.rs\ntests/nan_masking.rs\ntests/phase_b_configs.rs\ntests/phase_c_errors.rs\ntests/reachability.rs\ntests/smoke.rs\nverify.sh\n\n=== symbol diff (must be empty) ===\nIDENTICAL","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"188fe3ed-21de-4d11-9f80-120acb59131f","uuid":"0e444065-2741-494e-b81d-a910e208f38a","timestamp":"2026-08-19T15:12:53.684Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"=== Cargo.toml ===\n[package]\nname = \"translated_rust\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n[lib]\nname = \"tfm_lib\"\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\npath = \"src/lib.rs\"\n\n[features]\ndefault = []\n\n[dev-dependencies]\nlibloading = \"0.8\"\n\n[profile.release]\npanic = \"abort\"\n\n[workspace]\n\n=== deliverables ===\nCONFIGS.md\nERRORS.md\nSYMBOLS.md\nVERIFICATION.md\nmutation_check.sh\ntests/common/mod.rs\ntests/fuzz_explore.rs\ntests/nan_masking.rs\ntests/phase_b_configs.rs\ntests/phase_c_errors.rs\ntests/reachability.rs\ntests/smoke.rs\nverify.sh\n\n=== symbol diff (must be empty) ===\nIDENTICAL","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_voag7zfcjbgu7bdgq5ollsb2davbjsjprl7ipy4fst44df7upina","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src/` is a single 32-line translation unit exporting one function:\n\n```c\nvoid tfm(float *dest, const float *src, int count);\n```\n\nIt reads triples `(src[0], src[1], src[2])`, picks one of two asymmetric arms on `src[0] < src[1]`, computes the smaller eigenvalue-style quantity `lambda` via an inlined `MAX(0, sqd)` and `sqrtf`, and writes two floats per triple.\n\n## Phase A — surface mapped\n\n| artifact | content |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | `nm -D`: C exports `tfm`; Rust exports `tfm`. **Symbol diff empty, 0 non-libc undefined.** No C source was left untranslated — `CMakeLists.txt` lists one file, which declares one function. |\n| `ERRORS.md` | **28 rows** (29 with 21a/21b), mechanically grepped. `tfm` has *no* `return`, `assert`, `NULL` check or `errno` — so rows cover the three control-flow guards and every IEEE-754 invalid-op site. |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | **34 rows** across the axes the C actually branches on: `count` (negative/0/1/2/1024/200 000), both arms, clamp taken/not, every `f32` class, and 5 memory shapes (disjoint, exact alias, both overlap directions, byte-unaligned). |\n\nBuild-time surface: `Cargo.toml` has no features beyond an empty `default`; the C has no `#ifdef`/`option()`. `verify.sh` derives the feature power set from `Cargo.toml` mechanically and runs every combination in both `dev` and `release`.\n\n## Phases B–D — results\n\n**74 tests, all passing**, in every feature combination × both profiles. Every row of both tables is checked off by a dedicated test that drives `tfm` through `libloading` on **both** `.so` files and compares raw `u32` bit patterns plus guard words.\n\n**No Rust fixes were needed** — so I worked hard to prove that isn't a vacuous pass:\n\n- **`./mutation_check.sh`** injects 29 plausible mis-translations. **21 behavioural ones are all caught** (commuted final addition, `f32::max` instead of the C ternary, `0x7fc00000` instead of x86's `0xffc00000`, source- vs destination-operand NaN propagation, `<=` for `<`, unsigned `count`, wrong strides, each wrong constant…). The other **8 are declared equivalent, and each justification is itself asserted against the C `.so`** in `tests/nan_masking.rs` — e.g. when both `src[0]` and `src[1]` are NaN the C provably returns `(src[2], src[1] | 0x00400000)` regardless of `lambda` (187 264 cases), which is why four operand-order variants are unobservable.\n- **`tests/reachability.rs`** maintains the reachable/unreachable map behind the tables and fails if it goes stale. Five documented conditions (`sqd == -0.0`, `0*inf` in `4*dxy*dxy`, negative `sqrtf` argument, `lambda == -inf`, `dx2 - lambda` both-infinite) are **proven unreachable** with 0 hits over 2M+ inputs — those rows use `assert_unreachable()`, which still differentially checks the whole search space rather than passing vacuously.\n- An **independent C driver** (`dlopen` both `.so`s, no Rust harness) found **0 differences in 40 000 000 output elements**, for both the debug and release cdylib.\n\n## One thing worth flagging\n\nThe reference is inherently `-O0`, because `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` sets no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`. I measured the same C source at `-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os`: they differ from `-O0` in **0.01–0.02 % of elements, all of them NaN payloads, with 0 non-NaN value differences** over 8M elements per level. `fadd`/`fmul` are commutative for values but not for SSE NaN payloads, so the optimizer may pick either operand order. The Rust pins the `-O0` order. If the C is ever built with optimization, only NaN payloads would diverge; all numeric results are `-O`-independent. This is documented in `VERIFICATION.md`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":823,"cache_read_input_tokens":198899,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":823,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"188fe3ed-21de-4d11-9f80-120acb59131f","uuid":"4e8cfecc-9958-4e95-b917-24e7b2aab65c","timestamp":"2026-08-19T15:13:15.295Z"}
